### 4.5 Detector Efficiency

#### TPC Efficiency

In real case, as we don' t have 100% detector efficiency (include acceptance), we need to estimate the effect of the detector efficiency on the observable. The TPC efficiencies as a function of transverse momentum for identified particles can be calculated from the embedding simulation.

\[\epsilon(p_{T})=\frac{N_{RC}(p_{T})}{N_{MC}(p_{T})}, \tag{4.1}\]

where \(N_{RC}\) and \(N_{MC}\) represent the number of reconstruct track and Monte Carlo tracks, respectively. Figure 4.16 shows the TPC efficiency as a function of \(p_{T}\), which is well fitted by the function:

\[y=p_{0}e^{-(\frac{p_{1}}{x})^{p_{2}}} \tag{4.2}\]

Figure 4.16: (a) \(p_{T}\) dependence of the embedding efficiency for proton at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) GeV. The red line is the fitting function according to Eq. (4.2). (b) \(p_{T}\) dependence of the embedding efficiency for pion at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) GeV.

#### TOF Efficiency

The TOF matching efficiency is calculated using a data driven method and can be calculated as:

\[\epsilon(p_{T})=\frac{\text{The number of TOF matched track (MatchFlag}>0\text{, Track cuts, }|Nsigma|<2)}{\text{The number of TPC tracks (Track cuts, }|Nsigma|<2)}. \tag{4.3}\]

Figure 4.17 shows the \(p_{T}\) dependence of the TOF match efficiency for protons and pions.

#### TPC+TOF Efficiency

The particle identification method is different between low- and high-\(p_{T}\) regions. The TPC detector is used to obtain momentum of charged particles and do the particle identification in low-\(p_{T}\) region. Moreover, the TOF detector is used to do the particle identification in the relatively-high-\(p_{T}\) region. In this case, particles need to be counted separately for the two

