Figure 5.25 shows the energy dependence of \(\nu\) of charged hadrons in Au + Au collisions for two collision centralities (0-5% and 10-40%). In the most central collisions, \(\nu\) exhibits a non-monotonic behavior on collision energy and seems to reach a minimum around \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 20-30 GeV. On the other hand, \(\nu\) shows a flat trend with increasing \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) in 10-40% central collisions. At lower \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)\(\leq\) 14.5 GeV, statistical and systematic uncertainty for \(\nu\) are large, higher statistics data from BES-II will help confirm the trend of energy dependence of \(\nu\). Compare to theoretical prediction of critical \(\nu=1.30\) from GL theory [69] and 1.0 from 2D-Ising model [70, 75] which both are given in entire phase space and whole acceptance level, the observed \(\nu\) is much smaller. Of course, it also much smaller than calculations from AMPT [77] and UrQMD [105] models. It is note that the observed \(\nu\) is measured in available region of transverse momentum space with \(\eta\) and \(p_{T}\) acceptance, critical \(\nu\) under such conditions need to be pointed out in theory.

